*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** This is where you will report your $15,000 IT contracting income. You will deduct your $1,350 in work supplies (if applicable to the 1099 work) here.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will use this form to calculate your ~$5,377+ actual-expense home office deduction, which then flows onto your Schedule C.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** You must pay Medicare and Social Security taxes on your net 1099 profit (Income minus Schedule C expenses and Home Office deduction). 
*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** This is where your net business income from Schedule C goes. It is also where you will claim your **$2,500 Student Loan Interest Deduction** and a deduction for half of your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** This is where your calculated Self-Employment tax from Schedule SE is attached to your main return.

**Summary of your strategy:** Claim the $27,700 standard deduction, deduct $2,500 for student loan interest above-the-line, and aggressively lower your $15,000 self-employment income by claiming your work supplies and using the Actual Expenses method for your home office (Form 8829).
